Q: Where is Kafka data stored?

A: On the **disk of Kafka brokers** (commit log files), not as “only in memory” and not in ZooKeeper/KRaft metadata store.

Structure:
Topic (e.g. orders)
  └── Partition P0, P1, ...
        └── log segment files on broker disk
            (e.g. under log.dirs like /var/kafka-logs/orders-0/)

Important points:
1) Consumers reading a message does NOT delete it.
2) Data stays until retention expires (time and/or size), e.g. 7 days.
3) Kafka is a durable log, so replay is possible within retention.

Interview nuance (good extra):
Kafka is disk-based, but it heavily uses the OS **page cache**, so reads/writes often feel memory-fast.
Still: source of truth = disk log on brokers.

Not stored in:
- ZooKeeper / KRaft → cluster metadata only
- Consumer app memory → temporary processing only
- Your MySQL/Postgres → unless you write there yourself in consumer code

One-liner:
“Kafka stores topic partition data as append-only log files on broker disks, retained by policy even after consume; page cache makes it fast.”
